<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elche</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elx</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elche is a city in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, near the site of Iberian and Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-alcudia-elche">Ilici</a> at La Alcudia, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche">Lady of Elche</a> was found. Its Palm Grove, planted in the Islamic period, and its medieval mystery play, the Misteri d'Elx, are both recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>. It is now a centre of footwear manufacture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elche.jpg" alt="Elche" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:36272-Elche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The neighbouring site of La Alcudia, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-alcudia-elche">Ilici</a>, had consolidated its urban structure by the early 5th century BCE and became the centre of power of a local Iberian group. Under Roman rule it was a colonia connected to maritime transport through the Portus Ilicitanus, and in late antiquity it became a bishopric. After the Treaty of Tudmir, and especially when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> became the new centre of Umayyad power in the 9th century, the site declined. A new Islamic city, Madinat Ilš, was founded on the present site, with walls dated between the second half of the 10th century and the first third of the 11th, refurbished in the 12th century under Ibn Mardanīsh, who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Murcia.</p><p>In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and was then conquered by Aragonese forces on behalf of Castile. The disputed rule over Elche was settled in favour of the Crown of Aragon by the Sentence of Torrellas in 1304 and the Treaty of Elche in 1305, and it joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. In the 13th and 14th centuries the population remained mainly Muslim, and Elche also had a small but noted Jewish community. Townspeople opposed the creation of a lordship of Elche in 1470 and took part in the early 16th-century Revolt of the Brotherhoods. About 1563 the moriscos were 40 percent of the population, and an anti-seigneurial revolt broke out in the spring of 1766.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Elche is a city and municipality in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, in the Valencian Community. King Amadeo granted it the title of city in 1871. The espadrille industry developed in the 19th century, and local entrepreneurs later invested in leather footwear, so that the city became the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s footwear industry. The Palm Grove, an urban orchard of more than 200,000 palm trees, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2000. It is the only palm grove in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with North African origins and the largest on the continent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Misteri d'Elx, a sacral-lyrical medieval drama dated from the 15th century, is performed every year in mid-August in the festivities of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary. UNESCO declared it a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2002. The Alejandro Ramos Folqués Archaeological and History Museum, in the Altamira Palace, shows the city's past from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> to the Islamic settlement, and holds finds from La Alcudia, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche">Lady of Elche</a> was found. The Basilica of Santa Maria was built in 1672, and the Calahorra Tower is the last relic of the old city walls.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their towns, such as Ilici (La Alcudia, <b>Elche</b>), La Serreta near Alcoy and Lucentum, were centres of a refined Iberian culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/contestani">Contestani</a></li></ul></section>
